  "query_preview": "..\n\nShortwave ridging quickly departs to the east early this morning as \na weak mid-level wave reaches lower MI. While the influence of said \nridge was brief, its primary impact has been significant dry \nadvection with PW holding around 0.25\". Arriving mid-level shortwave \nstruggles to overcome the now resident airmass limiting overall \nshower coverage/longevity resulting in a dry, seasonable day for the \nmajority of the CWA today. The area that stands the best chance \n(~25%) at seeing a few light showers this afternoon is the Saginaw \nValley owing to their closer proximity to the wave and better column \nsaturation.\n\nActive pattern returns by Sunday as a Pacific NW originating trough \nreaches the Ohio Valley. Surface low pressure still favored to lift \nnortheastward across OH placing SE MI under its northern flank for \nmuch of the day. Models have trended slightly earlier with the lead \narm of isentropic ascent, reaching the area by late Sunday morning, \nsupporting increasing coverage of light showers. Intensities quickly \nramp up by afternoon and continue into the evening tied to the \nperiod of strongest vorticity advection as well as the arrival of \nthe left exit region of the Ohio valley jet streak. Colder airmass \n(supportive of snow) dropping out of Canada is lagged from the low \npassage, arriving over lower MI late Sunday night just as the low \nand associated precip peels away from the central Great Lakes. \nBecause of this, forecast soundings still advertise wet bulb zero \nheights in the 3-5kft range throughout nearly the entirety of the \nprecipitation window keeping p-type as a cold rain. A transition to \nrain-snow mix or all snow is possible near the end of the \nprecipitation window as colder air finally arrives however this \nwould be brief with no little to no accumulations. QPF range remains \nbetween 0.25-0.75\" with most seeing around 0.5\". Highest totals in \nthe range occur towards the south/southeastern-most portions of the \nCWA with the lowest towards the north/northwestern-most portions. \n\nIncreasing mixing depths within backend cold advection likely \nsupport gustier northwesterly winds (25-35mph) late Sunday night \nthrough the first part of Monday keeping wind chills in the lower \n20s. Slightly below normal temps (upper 30s/near 40) start the new \nwork week as thermal troughing settles over central Great Lakes. \nModeration in temperatures arrive by midweek as Ohio valley surface \nridge axis shifts towards the Northeast setting up southerly return \nflow as upper flow over the central CONUS turns more zonal. Long \nduration of south low level flow leads to persistent warm advection \ninto lower MI potentially pushing highs well above normal in the 50s \nby late week.",
